Q7)  What is LinkedList?
A LinkedList is a linear data structure where elements are stored in separate objects called nodes.
Unlike an ArrayList, elements are not stored in a continuous array.

*******************Each node contains:

[ Previous | Data | Next ]

*******************LinkedList Structure

LinkedList<Integer> list = new LinkedList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
list.add(30);


********************Conceptually, its structure is:
        Node 1              Node 2              Node 3

     ┌────────────┐      ┌────────────┐      ┌────────────┐
     │ prev       │      │ prev       │      │ prev       │
     │  null      │      │  Node 1    │      │  Node 2    │
     │            │      │            │      │            │
     │ data = 10  │ ───► │ data = 20  │ ───► │ data = 30  │
     │            │      │            │      │            │
     │ next       │      │ next       │      │ next       │
     │  Node 2    │ ◄─── │  Node 3    │ ◄─── │  null      │
     └────────────┘      └────────────┘      └────────────┘

null ← [10] ⇄ [20] ⇄ [30] → null

Q1) Why is it called "Linked" List?
Because nodes are linked with references.        //[10] ⇄ [20] ⇄ [30]

***************************Java LinkedList Syntax
LinkedList<DataType> variableName = new LinkedList<>();

Q2) "What is the internal structure of Java LinkedList?"

"Java LinkedList is implemented as a doubly linked list. Each node contains the element, a reference to the previous node, and a reference to the next node."

Q3) why we use linked list or doubly list if they have same method ?

"Although ArrayList and LinkedList implement the same List interface and provide similar operations, their internal implementations are different. ArrayList is backed by a dynamic array, so it provides fast random access by index. 

LinkedList is a doubly linked list, so index-based access is slower, but operations at the ends and unlinking a known node are efficient. Therefore, developers choose between them based on the application's access pattern."

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is the difference between ArrayList and LinkedList?


"ArrayList internally uses a dynamic array, whereas LinkedList uses a doubly linked list. ArrayList provides fast random access using index, so get operation is O(1). 

LinkedList requires traversal for index-based access, so get is O(n). LinkedList is useful when we frequently insert or remove elements, especially at the beginning or end. ArrayList is generally preferred when we frequently access or search elements by index."

